            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Gotchas",
          "text": "- Ce is for masculine nouns before a consonant (ce top); cet is for masculine before a vowel or silent h (cet imperméable, cet homme), because ce + vowel would be hard to say. Cette is feminine and never changes: cette jupe, cette écharpe.\n- Cet and cette sound exactly the same (sɛt), so you hear the gender only from the noun. Ces is plural for both genders: ces pulls, ces jupes.  Before a vowel the s is pronounced z: ces imperméables.\n- French has only one word for “this” and “that”. To make the difference, add -ci (here) or -là (there) after the noun: ce pull-ci (this one) / ce pull-là (that one).\n- Sous and sur are easy to mix up: sous = under, sur = on. They sound different: sous [su] has the “oo” of “soon”, sur [syʁ] has the French u (round your lips as for “oo” and say “ee”).\n- Entre is always followed by two things joined with et: entre le pull et la jupe.\n- Devant and derrière are followed directly by the noun: devant la porte, derrière le magasin. You do not need de."
        },
