        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Gotchas",
          "text": "- Most adjectives follow the noun, as in English they would come before: une robe magnifique, un pantalon décontracté (relaxed, casual), un pull confortable.\n- A few short, very common adjectives go before the noun: beau, joli, grand, petit, bon, nouveau, vieux, jeune. Memory trick: Beauty, Age, Goodness, Size (BAGS).\n- Beau changes before a masculine vowel: un bel imperméable (not “beau”). Feminine: belle; plural: beaux / belles. A famous trap: un beau pull (consonant), un bel anorak.\n- Between an adjective and a plural noun, des becomes de (more formal): de belles chaussures, de grands magasins. In everyday speech, des is common: des belles chaussures.\n- Essayer is a regular -er verb with an optional spelling change: y becomes i before a silent ending (j'essaie, tu essaies, il essaie, ils essaient), but not before the nous / vous endings (essayons, essayez). The y form (j'essaye) is also correct.\n- Essayer = to try, to try on. J'essaie ce pantalon. And Essayer de + infinitive: J'essaie de comprendre."
        },
